## Why This Is Asked

Webhooks are central to reliable integrations in payments; testing ensures resilience to retries, dedup, and partial failures, which beginner testers can demonstrate through practical CI plans.

## Key Concepts

- At-least-once delivery
- Idempotency
- Retry strategies and jitter
- End-to-end testing with mocks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: webhook sink with idempotency key check
const deliveries = new Map();
function deliver(ctx) {
  const key = ctx.idempotencyKey;
  if (deliveries.has(key)) return 'duplicate';
  deliveries.set(key, true);
  return 'delivered';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test out-of-order deliveries?
- How would you simulate a non-idempotent endpoint handle correctly?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for event-driven flows are common in production; beginners should grasp end-to-end testing, idempotency, and failure handling.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing of queues and email sinks
- Idempotent processing of events
- Retries, backoff, and dead-letter queues
- Isolation of external dependencies in CI

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple idempotent handler skeleton
const seen = new Set();
function handleEvent(event){
  if (seen.has(event.id)) return;
  seen.add(event.id);
  sendEmail(event.userEmail, 'Welcome!', {eventId: event.id});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the dead-letter path for poison messages?
- How would you simulate email service outage and verify retry/backoff behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical handling of feature flags, data-driven tests, and CI reliability for a common pattern in product risk: toggles affecting user-visible behavior.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and governance
- Deterministic test data
- API-contract testing between components
- Test isolation and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: deterministic ranking seed and test
function rank(dataset, algo) {
  // simplified placeholder for ranking logic
  return dataset.map(item => item.theta).sort();
}
const seed = [
  { q: 'apple', theta: 3 },
  { q: 'apple', theta: 1 },
  { q: 'apple', theta: 2 }
];
const resultA = rank(seed, 'algoA');
const resultB = rank(seed, 'algoB');
